<!-- ============================================================
     Microsoft SharePoint Server Deserialization of Untrusted Data RCE
     CVE-2026-58644 (CWE-502: Deserialization of Untrusted Data)
     Affected: Microsoft SharePoint Server (multiple supported versions)
     MITRE ATT&amp;CK:
       T1190 - Exploit Public-Facing Application (TA0001 Initial Access)
       T1059.001 - Command and Scripting Interpreter: PowerShell (TA0002 Execution)
       T1059.003 - Command and Scripting Interpreter: Windows Command Shell (TA0002)
       T1210 - Exploitation of Remote Services (TA0008 Lateral Movement)
     CISA KEV: Added 2026-07-16, Federal Deadline 2026-07-19
     Rule IDs: 103979-103985
     Author: SecOps Engineer (Purple Team)
     Date: 2026-07-16

     Vulnerability Summary:

     CVE-2026-58644 is a critical deserialization of untrusted data vulnerability
     (CWE-502) in Microsoft SharePoint Server. An unauthenticated network attacker
     can send a specially crafted HTTP request containing a malicious serialized
     .NET object to SharePoint web service endpoints. SharePoint deserializes the
     payload using the BinaryFormatter or equivalent .NET deserializer without
     adequate type filtering, allowing the attacker to supply a ysoserial.net-style
     gadget chain. Successful exploitation results in arbitrary code execution
     under the IIS application pool identity (NETWORK SERVICE or a dedicated
     SharePoint service account).

     CISA added CVE-2026-58644 to the Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog on
     2026-07-16, indicating confirmed in-the-wild exploitation with a July 19
     federal remediation deadline.

     Attack chain:
       1. Attacker sends an HTTP POST to a SharePoint CSOM or SOAP endpoint
          (/_vti_bin/client.svc, /_vti_bin/*.asmx) with a crafted serialized
          .NET payload in the request body.
       2. SharePoint deserializes the payload without validating the contained
          types, invoking a ysoserial.net gadget chain (TypeConfuseDelegate,
          WindowsIdentity, or similar).
       3. The gadget chain triggers code execution under the IIS application
          pool process (w3wp.exe) identity.
       4. Attacker achieves arbitrary command execution, typically staging a
          reverse shell or downloading additional tooling.

     Detection Approach:

     HTTP / Traefik reverse-proxy layer (rules 103979-103983):
       Rule 103979 (level 8): Base anchor - any request to SharePoint CSOM or
         SOAP endpoint (/_vti_bin/client.svc or /_vti_bin/*.asmx). These endpoints
         process client-supplied serialized data and represent the primary attack
         surface for CVE-2026-58644.
       Rule 103980 (level 11): POST to SharePoint CSOM or SOAP endpoint (attack
         method). GET requests to these endpoints are routine health checks; POST
         is the deserialization exploit delivery method.
       Rule 103981 (level 13): POST to SharePoint CSOM endpoint returning HTTP 2xx
         (deserialization was executed). A successful response to an unauthenticated
         crafted POST to client.svc is a high-confidence exploitation signal.
       Rule 103982 (level 13): POST to SharePoint SOAP endpoint (/_vti_bin/*.asmx)
         returning HTTP 2xx from a non-internal source. SOAP services process
         XML-encoded serialized data and may also be vulnerable to the
         CVE-2026-58644 deserialization path.
       Rule 103983 (level 9): Probe burst - 8 or more POST requests to SharePoint
         CSOM or SOAP endpoints from the same source IP within 60 seconds.
         Automated exploit tooling generates rapid probe patterns while fuzzing
         deserialization gadget chains.

     IIS / Sysmon process layer (rules 103984-103985):
       Rule 103984 (level 14): Sysmon EventID 1 - IIS w3wp.exe spawning a
         suspicious child process (cmd.exe, powershell.exe, csc.exe, mshta.exe,
         wscript.exe, cscript.exe). When CVE-2026-58644 deserialization succeeds,
         the gadget chain typically invokes a subprocess under the IIS worker
         process. Any child process spawned by w3wp.exe that is not expected
         in normal SharePoint operation warrants investigation.
       Rule 103985 (level 15): Sysmon EventID 1 - w3wp.exe spawning download or
         staging tools (certutil, bitsadmin, curl, wget, msiexec for remote
         install). These are high-confidence indicators of post-exploitation
         payload staging following successful RCE via CVE-2026-58644.

     Log source prerequisites:
       HTTP layer:
         Traefik access logs in JSON format forwarded to Wazuh.
         Field names: RequestMethod, RequestPath, RequestHost, DownstreamStatus,
         ClientHost. SharePoint must be behind the Traefik reverse proxy.
       IIS/Sysmon layer:
         Sysmon EventID 1 (Process Create) forwarded to Wazuh via Windows agent.
         Requires Sysmon installed on SharePoint server with process creation
         logging enabled. Wazuh Windows agent decodes Sysmon events.
         Field names: win.eventdata.parentImage, win.eventdata.image,
         win.eventdata.commandLine.

     Severity tiers:
        8 - Any request to SharePoint CSOM or SOAP endpoint (info anchor)
       11 - POST to CSOM or SOAP endpoint (attack delivery method)
       13 - POST to client.svc returning 2xx (deserialization processed)
       13 - POST to SOAP endpoint returning 2xx from external source
        9 - Probe burst: 8+ POST requests to CSOM/SOAP from same IP in 60 seconds
       14 - w3wp.exe spawning cmd/PowerShell/script interpreter (possible RCE)
       15 - w3wp.exe spawning download/staging tool (confirmed post-exploit staging)

     False positive guidance:
       103979: SharePoint CSOM is used by legitimate SharePoint client applications
         (SharePoint Designer, Office clients, PowerShell PnP). Baseline known-good
         client application IPs and suppress via overrides.
       103980: POST to client.svc from trusted SharePoint client tools is expected;
         review ClientHost against authorized client IP ranges. External or
         internet-sourced POSTs have no legitimate business justification.
       103981: A 2xx response to a POST from a legitimate SharePoint client is
         expected behavior; flag requests from unknown or external source IPs.
       103982: SOAP services may be called by legacy SharePoint integrations;
         review ClientHost. Unexpected external source IPs should be investigated.
       103983: SharePoint data migration or bulk operation tools may generate
         rapid sequential CSOM requests; correlate source IP with known tools.
       103984: Some SharePoint workflows legitimately invoke PowerShell scripts
         via w3wp.exe; review the specific command line in win.eventdata.commandLine
         against known authorized SharePoint PowerShell automation.
       103985: No legitimate SharePoint workflow invokes certutil, bitsadmin,
         curl, or wget from w3wp.exe; treat as confirmed post-exploit staging
         requiring immediate incident response.
     ============================================================ -->

<group name="initial_access,execution,exploit_pubfacing,sharepoint,cisa_kev,cve_2026_58644,deserialization,">

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Layer 1: HTTP / Traefik reverse proxy (JSON access logs)
       Rule IDs: 103979-103983

       CVE-2026-58644 is exploited by sending a crafted deserialization
       payload via HTTP POST to SharePoint CSOM or SOAP web service
       endpoints. Monitoring the reverse proxy layer catches exploit
       delivery before post-exploit host-level events fire.
       =========================================================== -->

  <!-- Base anchor: any request to SharePoint CSOM or SOAP web service endpoint.

       SharePoint CSOM (Client Side Object Model) at /_vti_bin/client.svc is
       the primary attack surface for CVE-2026-58644 deserialization. This
       endpoint accepts client-supplied serialized objects for server-side
       processing. The /_vti_bin/*.asmx SOAP endpoints also handle
       serialized XML payloads and may share the vulnerable deserialization
       code path.

       Any unexpected access to these endpoints from external or non-whitelisted
       source IPs should be investigated. This anchor is level 8 (informational
       correlation only). All child rules (103980-103983) chain from this rule. -->
  <rule id="103979" level="8">
    <decoded_as>json</decoded_as>
    <field name="RequestPath" type="pcre2">(?i)/_vti_bin/(?:client\.svc|[^/]+\.asmx)</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-58644: Request to SharePoint CSOM/SOAP endpoint $(RequestPath) from $(ClientHost) at $(RequestHost) -- CVE-2026-58644 deserialization attack surface anchor; correlate with child rules 103980-103985 (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_58644,deserialization,initial_access,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- POST method to SharePoint CSOM or SOAP endpoint: elevated suspicion.

       CVE-2026-58644 is exploited exclusively via HTTP POST. The deserialization
       vulnerability is triggered by a crafted POST body containing a malicious
       serialized .NET object (BinaryFormatter payload). GET requests to these
       endpoints occur during normal SharePoint client connectivity probes and
       health checks. POST requests from non-whitelisted sources or external
       IPs carrying large or unexpected bodies should trigger investigation. -->
  <rule id="103980" level="11">
    <if_sid>103979</if_sid>
    <field name="RequestMethod">POST</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-58644: POST to SharePoint CSOM/SOAP endpoint $(RequestPath) from $(ClientHost) at $(RequestHost) -- POST is the CVE-2026-58644 deserialization exploit delivery method; review source IP against authorized SharePoint client allowlist (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_58644,deserialization,post_method,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- POST to SharePoint CSOM endpoint returning HTTP 2xx: deserialization executed.

       Under a correctly patched SharePoint instance, deserialization of untrusted
       objects from unauthenticated or unauthorized callers is blocked. A 2xx
       response to a POST on /_vti_bin/client.svc from an unexpected source
       indicates SharePoint accepted and deserialized the payload, confirming
       the CVE-2026-58644 vulnerability is present and exploitable.

       This is a high-confidence exploitation signal. The source IP, request
       body size, and timestamp should be captured for incident response. -->
  <rule id="103981" level="13">
    <if_sid>103980</if_sid>
    <field name="RequestPath" type="pcre2">(?i)/_vti_bin/client\.svc</field>
    <field name="DownstreamStatus" type="pcre2">^2\d{2}$</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-58644: POST to SharePoint CSOM client.svc from $(ClientHost) returned HTTP $(DownstreamStatus) at $(RequestHost) -- server deserialized client payload; high-confidence CVE-2026-58644 exploitation; investigate immediately (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_58644,deserialization,post_method,auth_bypass,high_confidence,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- POST to SharePoint SOAP endpoint returning 2xx: RCE vector triggered.

       SharePoint SOAP web services (/_vti_bin/Lists.asmx, /_vti_bin/UserGroup.asmx,
       /_vti_bin/UserProfileService.asmx, etc.) process XML-encoded requests that
       may share the CVE-2026-58644 vulnerable deserialization path. A 2xx response
       to a POST on any /_vti_bin/*.asmx endpoint is suspicious when originating
       from external or unrecognized source IPs; legitimate SOAP integrations
       originate from known internal systems on defined schedules. -->
  <rule id="103982" level="13">
    <if_sid>103980</if_sid>
    <field name="RequestPath" type="pcre2">(?i)/_vti_bin/[^/]+\.asmx</field>
    <field name="DownstreamStatus" type="pcre2">^2\d{2}$</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-58644: POST to SharePoint SOAP service $(RequestPath) from $(ClientHost) returned HTTP $(DownstreamStatus) at $(RequestHost) -- SOAP endpoint processed deserialized payload; potential CVE-2026-58644 RCE path; correlate with host-layer events (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_58644,deserialization,soap_endpoint,post_method,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Probe burst: 8 or more POST requests to SharePoint CSOM/SOAP from same IP in 60 seconds.

       Automated CVE-2026-58644 exploitation tooling (ysoserial.net scripts,
       Metasploit modules) typically sends rapid sequential POST requests while
       iterating over gadget chains, payloads, or target endpoints. A burst of
       8 or more requests from a single source IP to these endpoints in 60 seconds
       has no legitimate explanation in normal SharePoint client operation. -->
  <rule id="103983" level="9" frequency="8" timeframe="60">
    <if_matched_sid>103979</if_matched_sid>
    <same_field>ClientHost</same_field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-58644: Probe burst - $(ClientHost) sent 8+ requests to SharePoint CSOM/SOAP endpoints at $(RequestHost) within 60 seconds -- automated exploit tooling fingerprint; investigate source IP for CVE-2026-58644 scanning (T1190)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_58644,probe_burst,scanner,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- ===========================================================
       Layer 2: IIS / Sysmon process creation (Windows endpoint)
       Rule IDs: 103984-103985

       Successful CVE-2026-58644 exploitation causes the SharePoint IIS
       worker process (w3wp.exe) to spawn an attacker-controlled subprocess.
       Sysmon EventID 1 (Process Create) captures this parent-child
       relationship. These rules detect the post-exploitation phase where
       the deserialization gadget chain has already succeeded.

       Sysmon Wazuh SID 61603 covers Process Create events.
       =========================================================== -->

  <!-- Sysmon EventID 1: IIS w3wp.exe spawning suspicious child process.

       Under normal SharePoint operation, w3wp.exe (the IIS application pool
       worker process) does not spawn interactive command interpreters or
       script engines directly. CVE-2026-58644 deserialization gadget chains
       commonly invoke:
         - cmd.exe: direct shell command execution
         - powershell.exe: PowerShell-based staging or payload download
         - csc.exe: C# compiler for in-memory code compilation
         - mshta.exe: HTML Application host for script execution
         - wscript.exe / cscript.exe: Windows Script Host execution
       Any of these processes with w3wp.exe as parent is a strong indicator
       that deserialization-triggered code execution has occurred. -->
  <rule id="103984" level="14">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.parentImage" type="pcre2">(?i)\\w3wp\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(?:cmd|powershell|csc|mshta|wscript|cscript)\.exe$</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-58644: IIS w3wp.exe spawned $(win.eventdata.image) on $(agent.name) -- command interpreter child of IIS worker process indicates deserialization RCE; review commandLine for exploit payload; CVE-2026-58644 post-exploitation (T1190/T1059)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
      <id>T1059.003</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_58644,execution,iis_exploit,deserialization_rce,high_confidence,</group>
  </rule>

  <!-- Sysmon EventID 1: w3wp.exe spawning download or payload staging tools.

       Following successful CVE-2026-58644 deserialization RCE, attackers
       typically stage a persistent payload or reverse shell via download
       utilities. These tools are not invoked by w3wp.exe in any normal
       SharePoint operation:
         - certutil.exe: commonly used for base64 decode and remote download
         - bitsadmin.exe: Background Intelligent Transfer Service download
         - curl.exe: HTTP download from attacker-controlled server
         - msiexec.exe: remote MSI package installation via URL
         - regsvr32.exe: COM scriptlet execution or DLL registration
       Presence of any of these as a child of w3wp.exe is a confirmed
       post-exploitation payload staging indicator requiring immediate
       incident response. -->
  <rule id="103985" level="15">
    <if_sid>61603</if_sid>
    <field name="win.eventdata.parentImage" type="pcre2">(?i)\\w3wp\.exe$</field>
    <field name="win.eventdata.image" type="pcre2">(?i)\\(?:certutil|bitsadmin|curl|msiexec|regsvr32|rundll32|regasm|installutil)\.exe$</field>
    <description>SharePoint CVE-2026-58644 CRITICAL: IIS w3wp.exe spawned payload staging tool $(win.eventdata.image) on $(agent.name) -- confirmed post-exploitation payload download or staging; CVE-2026-58644 deserialization RCE with follow-on tool deployment; initiate incident response immediately (T1190/T1059/T1105)</description>
    <mitre>
      <id>T1190</id>
      <id>T1059.001</id>
      <id>T1105</id>
    </mitre>
    <group>sharepoint,cve_2026_58644,execution,iis_exploit,deserialization_rce,payload_staging,critical,</group>
  </rule>

</group>
